---
/**
 * /integrations: ported from the legacy `integrations.html`.
 *
 * ── OA-10 IS RESOLVED HERE, AND THE ACCURACY WORK IS THE PORT ───────────────
 *
 * OA-10 blocked this route: the hero claimed "Read-only throughout" in the same
 * sentence as "route alerts to your channels", and the page went on to offer
 * exports and SMS. The legacy source was already narrowed on 2026-08-02 to "We
 * read from the systems you connect and never write back to them", which is the
 * claim the privacy notice makes per connector and the one this page keeps.
 *
 * What was NOT fixed then, and is fixed here, is that the page named connectors
 * that do not exist and omitted every connector that does. Each change below
 * has its evidence in src/data/integrations.ts, file and line, in the platform
 * repo. Nothing is asserted from memory.
 *
 *   ADDED, and this is the largest correction. The hero promised "read
 *   documents where they live" and the page never named a single document
 *   source. SharePoint, OneDrive for Business and Confluence Cloud are the
 *   three the platform supports (content_sources.py:93), each with its real
 *   scope. They are the reason the read-only claim can be made at all, and they
 *   were the one thing missing.
 *
 *   REMOVED: "Send reminders from your own number", the Twilio / Vonage /
 *   MessageBird section. web/lib/sms/providers.ts exists, but its only consumer
 *   is web/lib/auth/platform-sms.ts, a platform-owned password-reset OTP on
 *   PLATFORM_SMS_* credentials. There is no route, no settings screen and no
 *   per-organisation credential entry anywhere in web/app, and
 *   specs/CROWAGENT-PLATFORM-2.1-SCOPE.md:152 records it as "Stub. Needs
 *   provider wiring". A customer cannot connect their own number today, so the
 *   section described something nobody can use. Recorded in
 *   check-content-parity.js.
 *
 *   REMOVED: the Zapier and Make logo pair under "Automation platforms".
 *   CONNECTOR_PROVIDER_CONFIG holds three providers and neither is one of them,
 *   and there is no Make module in the platform repo at all. The section's
 *   HEADING is kept, because what it promises is true by a different mechanism:
 *   rules deliver over a generic outbound webhook, which is what
 *   WorkflowBuilder.tsx:515-517 says in as many words. See the note on that
 *   section below, and OWNER-ACTIONS for the Zapier REST-Hook API that does
 *   exist and is not published here.
 *
 *   CORRECTED: identity, and it moves in the generous direction. The legacy
 *   page badged Okta, Ping, OneLogin and a self-hosted IdP "Via Entra ID
 *   federation" and said native SAML 2.0 was "on the roadmap, not shipped yet".
 *   It ships: web/app/api/auth/sso/_lib/saml-config.ts is a provider-agnostic
 *   SAML 2.0 Service Provider, with SCIM 2.0 at web/app/api/scim/v2/** and DNS
 *   domain verification. /pricing already publishes single sign-on as available,
 *   so the two pages disagreed and this one was the stale half. OIDC genuinely
 *   is not supported and is not claimed.
 *
 *   NARROWED: the automation rule triggers. The legacy step card offered three
 *   example events; two of them carry `executable: False` in
 *   workflow_engine.py. Only the deadline sweep is named.
 *
 * ── NO TIER IS ATTACHED TO ANYTHING ON THIS PAGE ────────────────────────────
 * Same discipline as pricing.astro: which plan includes single sign-on is a
 * commercial packaging question that cannot be answered from the repo, so it is
 * stated as existing and left there rather than guessed at.
 *
 * ── NO JAVASCRIPT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The legacy page carried GSAP, ScrollTrigger and a transformation module for
 * scroll reveals. Nothing here needs any of them: every name, every scope and
 * every claim is in the HTML and painted at first render.
 *
 * -- 2026-10-22: ON DESIGN SYSTEM VERSION 3 --
 * R285-WEB-ENTERPRISE-REDESIGN-01, strategy 0.7. Every connector, scope, note,
 * link and action is kept, on the v3 bands: PageHero, one SectionBand per
 * section (sources, identity, outbound, webhooks, rules, hand off) alternating
 * light and light alt, each grid the shared .ea-cards row, and the shared Close
 * carrying Start now and View all pricing. The scope line stays monospaced
 * because it is the evidence a security reviewer checks. The vendor marks stay
 * withdrawn (A-37, A-38): every connector carries the same neutral tile.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/evidenced/PageHero.astro';
import SectionBand from '../components/evidenced/SectionBand.astro';
import Action from '../components/evidenced/Action.astro';
import IconTile from '../components/evidenced/IconTile.astro';
import Icon from '../components/evidenced/Icon.astro';
import Close from '../components/evidenced/Close.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/ui/Breadcrumb.astro';
import { SIGNUP_URL } from '../data/app';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';
import { SOURCES, IDENTITY, OUTBOUND, RULE_STEPS } from '../data/integrations';
import { INTEGRATIONS_PLAN_LINE } from '../data/site-facts';
import { PRODUCT_NAME_BID } from '../lib/brand-names';
import '../styles/evidenced.css';

const title = 'Integrations | Velstrian';
/* The description keeps the narrowed claim: documents are pulled read-only,
   alerts go outward to Slack or Teams. */
const description =
  'Sign in with your work identity, pull bid documents read-only from where your team keeps them, and send deadline alerts to Slack or Teams.';

const TRAIL = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }];

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Integrations', path: '/integrations/' },
  ]),
];

const SSO_EXTRAS = [
  'SCIM 2.0 provisioning, so joiners and leavers are handled by your directory',
  'Domain verification by DNS record before a domain can be claimed',
  'Signed assertions required. An unsigned one is rejected rather than trusted',
  'Enforcement, so an organisation can require single sign-on and close the password route',
];

const WEBHOOK = [
  'A URL you set, on any host you control, with no app to install at either end',
  'An optional secret header, so the receiver can prove the request came from us',
  'The event and the record it fired on, as JSON, in the request body',
  'No inbound access: a webhook is something we send, never a way in',
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/integrations"
  image="/Assets/og/integrations.png?v=20260730"
  schema={schema}
  typeface="evidenced"
  headerTone="light"
>
  <PageHero
    id="int-title"
    bandId="hero"
    label="Connect your stack"
    title="Works with the tools you already run."
    lead="Sign in with your work identity, read documents where they live, and route alerts to your channels. We read from the systems you connect and never write back to them."
  >
    <div slot="before" class="ea-trail"><Breadcrumb trail={TRAIL} current="Integrations" /></div>
    <Action href={SIGNUP_URL}>Start now</Action>
    <Action href="/security/" variant="secondary">Read the security model</Action>
  </PageHero>

  {/* READ ONLY. Every scope printed here is quoted from the platform repo
      (data/integrations.ts carries the file and line), because a scope is
      something a security reviewer can check. */}
  <SectionBand
    tone="light"
    eyebrow="Document sources · read only"
    title="Read your documents where they live."
    standfirst={`Your answer library is already written. It is in the last thirty bids, sitting in the places your team already keeps them, and ${PRODUCT_NAME_BID} reads it there rather than asking you to upload it again.`}
    id="sources"
  >
    <ul class="ea-cards" role="list">
      {
        SOURCES.map((s) => (
          <li data-reveal>
            <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
              <IconTile name="document" />
              <h3 class="ea-h3">{s.name}</h3>
              <p class="int-scope">{s.scope}</p>
              <p>{s.note}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="int-note">
      Every scope above is a read permission. Your files stay exactly where they are, as they are, and your tenant
      settings stay as you set them. SharePoint and OneDrive are granted by your own Microsoft administrator, so you can
      see exactly what was consented to and withdraw it without asking us.
    </p>
  </SectionBand>

  <SectionBand
    tone="light-alt"
    eyebrow="Identity and SSO"
    title="Use the directory you already administer."
    standfirst="Single sign-on through any SAML 2.0 identity provider, with users provisioned and deprovisioned from your directory rather than from a spreadsheet."
    id="sso"
  >
    <ul class="ea-cards" role="list">
      {
        IDENTITY.map((s) => (
          <li data-reveal>
            <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
              <IconTile name="lock" />
              <h3 class="ea-h3">{s.name}</h3>
              <p class="int-scope">{s.scope}</p>
              <p>{s.note}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <div class="ea-card ea-tcard int-solo" data-reveal>
      <h3 class="ea-h3">What comes with it</h3>
      <ul class="int-ticks" role="list">
        {SSO_EXTRAS.map((t) => <li><Icon name="done" size={18} /><span>{t}</span></li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="int-note">
      There is no app to install at your end and no provider-specific path in our code. You give us an entity ID, a
      sign-on URL and a signing certificate, which is why the list above is examples rather than a limit. OIDC is not
      supported yet, and is on <a class="ea-link" href="/roadmap/">the roadmap</a> rather than in the product.
    </p>
  </SectionBand>

  {/* THE WRITE CONNECTORS, under their own heading and their own claim. They
      must never sit under the read-only guarantee above. */}
  <SectionBand
    tone="light"
    eyebrow="Collaboration · outbound"
    title="Alerts where your team works."
    standfirst="Push a deadline or a delivery-evidence reminder straight into the channel your team already checks, and write an evidence export into a folder you choose."
    id="collaboration"
  >
    <ul class="ea-cards" role="list">
      {
        OUTBOUND.map((s) => (
          <li data-reveal>
            <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
              <IconTile name="chat" />
              <h3 class="ea-h3">{s.name}</h3>
              <p class="int-scope">{s.scope}</p>
              <p>{s.note}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="int-note">
      These three write, which is the whole point of them, so they are named here rather than folded into the read-only
      list above. Each one goes outward to a channel or a folder you configure, and none of them is a source we read
      from. A connector is authorised per organisation and can be disconnected from the same screen it was connected on.
    </p>
  </SectionBand>

  {/* A generic webhook, in those words (WorkflowBuilder.tsx). Naming a
      platform here would claim a packaged app. */}
  <SectionBand
    tone="light-alt"
    eyebrow="Outbound webhooks"
    title={`Wire ${PRODUCT_NAME_BID} into the tools you already automate.`}
    standfirst={`A rule can post its event to any URL you own, so a deadline in ${PRODUCT_NAME_BID} can start a workflow in whatever your team already runs.`}
    id="automation-platforms"
  >
    <div class="ea-card ea-tcard int-solo" data-reveal>
      <h3 class="ea-h3">What the webhook gives you</h3>
      <ul class="int-ticks" role="list">
        {WEBHOOK.map((t) => <li><Icon name="done" size={18} /><span>{t}</span></li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="int-note">
      This is deliberately a plain HTTP POST rather than a badge for a named automation platform. Anything that accepts
      an incoming webhook can receive it, and nothing on this page claims a packaged integration we have not built.
    </p>
  </SectionBand>

  <SectionBand
    tone="light"
    eyebrow="Automation rules"
    title="Trigger, condition, action."
    standfirst="Build a rule in the app: pick an event, set the conditions that matter, and choose what happens. No engineering ticket required."
    id="rules"
  >
    <ol class="ea-cards" role="list">
      {
        RULE_STEPS.map((s) => (
          <li data-reveal>
            <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
              <p class="ea-label">{s.label}</p>
              <h3 class="ea-h3">{s.heading}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    <p class="int-note">
      One trigger runs today, and it is the deadline sweep: {PRODUCT_NAME_BID} checks once a day for tender deadlines
      inside the window you set and fires the rules that match. We add each further trigger to this page when it runs.
    </p>
  </SectionBand>

  {/* R285-WEB-CLAIMS-AUDIT-01, capabilities M7 and M8, and row I5: e-sourcing
      packaging (prepares, never lodges), the CRM routes and the MCP server. The
      plan line reads the integrations flag from site-facts.json. No price is
      printed here. */}
  <SectionBand
    tone="light-alt"
    eyebrow="E-sourcing, CRM and agents"
    title="Hand off to the systems that own the next step."
    standfirst={`${PRODUCT_NAME_BID} prepares the work and passes it on. It never lodges a bid for you.`}
    id="handoff"
  >
    <ul class="ea-cards ea-cards--2" role="list">
      <li data-reveal>
        <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
          <h3 class="ea-h3">E-sourcing portals</h3>
          <p>
            {PRODUCT_NAME_BID} prepares the submission pack for Jaggaer, Atamis and In-tend and checks it against the
            portal's requirements. You submit it, in the portal, under your own account.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li data-reveal>
        <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
          <h3 class="ea-h3">Salesforce and HubSpot</h3>
          <p>
            Push your bids into the CRM with the stage mapping you set, and pull CRM changes back into the bid. A change
            that would overwrite an edit in {PRODUCT_NAME_BID} is held for you to review rather than applied.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li data-reveal>
        <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
          <h3 class="ea-h3">MCP server</h3>
          <p>
            A read and write Model Context Protocol server, so your team can work with {PRODUCT_NAME_BID} from inside
            its own AI tooling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li data-reveal>
        <div class="ea-card ea-tcard">
          <h3 class="ea-h3">The Integrations package</h3>
          <p>{INTEGRATIONS_PLAN_LINE}</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBand>

  <Close
    title="Connect once, bid from one place."
    lead="Every connector reads only what it needs, cites the statute behind each figure, and keeps your data in the EU."
    primary={{ href: SIGNUP_URL, label: 'Start now' }}
    secondary={{ href: '/pricing/', label: 'View all pricing' }}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The scope line is the per-connector EVIDENCE, quoted from the platform, so
     it is set in the mono face and wraps anywhere: a scope such as
     ChannelMessage.Send has a floor width that would otherwise push the page
     sideways at 390. */
  .int-scope {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-ea-tiny);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ea-accent-ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The notes are read, so they sit on the band as prose at the copy measure. */
  .int-note {
    max-width: var(--ea-copy);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--ea-sub);
  }

  .int-solo {
    max-width: var(--ea-copy);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .int-ticks {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .int-ticks li {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-items: flex-start;
    color: var(--ea-sub);
  }

  .int-ticks :global(svg) {
    flex: none;
    margin-top: 3px;
    color: var(--ea-accent-ink);
  }
</style>
